{"author":"Rebar Bot Nava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"make verify on 3df0baf4e8b (03:11-05:09 PST) exited 2 with 3 failures, all caused by this change and now fixed:\n- test_opcert_service::test_completion_pass_spawn_ceiling (and test_opcert_key_copy_leak_tortoise, which nests it): the opcert job started 31 git processes against a ceiling of 30, because the reflog expiry ran on every write. Each git start costs 0.1-1.3 s on a loaded host. Fix: the expiry now runs at most once a day, tracked by a stamp file's mtime in the common git dir; a store with no stamp starts the clock instead of expiring at once. Git's shortest default expiry is 30 days, so a daily pass loses nothing. Approach step 1 in the description is updated to say so.\n- test_subprocess_env_repr_security: my new test built a subprocess env from **os.environ; it now uses tests/_subprocess_env.subprocess_env.\nAll 3 suites pass now; 23/23 maintenance and expiry tests pass (new: once-a-day, failed pass retried and not stamped, no-stamp store starts the clock). Amended as 141750acb93; make verify re-running."},"env_id":"492a5c71-6cda-48a6-8134-02ff6a672739","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1794489978069420001,"uuid":"c10917dd-8b02-4174-8a99-c5df20ea2367"}